description: v019's globally-pooled loss PLUS training on train + the non-held-out
  validation sequences. Two changes off v011.
hypothesis: 'The single most important fix in this repo''s history, and it was sitting
  in plain sight. This repo trained nineteen versions on a loss whose own docstring
  lied: chunk_loss() accepted a `state` argument and claimed it ''carries the moments
  across chunks so the statistic is cumulative rather than restarted''. The body never
  read `state`, and the call site never passed it. The weighted-Pearson statistic
  was therefore recomputed from each chunk''s own rows and restarted 20 times per
  sequence.


  That is exactly the failure dozken/wunder documents and calls out by name: a per-chunk
  Pearson subtracts a weighted mean estimated from that chunk alone, so the slow level
  component the metric actually scores is discarded and any per-chunk level offset
  is free. Their chunk4k runs ''win within-sequence correlation and lose on level
  drift'', and models trained on their chunk loss get WORSE with more training (0.575
  -> 0.553) instead of better. Their fix -- a running detached sufficient statistic
  pooled over the batch as well as over time -- is the 2026-09-15 metric, which pools
  every scored row of every sequence into ONE correlation per target.


  Expected: a large gain, larger than anything else we have tried, because every prior
  run was optimising a surrogate that structurally cannot see the quantity being scored.
  Our +0.058 over the organiser baseline came from the soft mask and the state reset
  on top of a mis-specified loss; with the loss correct those two should compound
  rather than merely survive.


  SECOND CHANGE: data. dozken train on train + non-held-out validation (`--add-valid`),
  which is 10,607 + 1,673 = 12,280 sequences, +15.8% more data. docs/faq.md permits
  it explicitly (''All solutions must be trained using only the provided train.parquet
  and valid.parquet'') and we never did it in any of nineteen runs. Evaluation stays
  clean: our eval subset is validation sequences 0..199, and cache/trainval holds
  only 200..1872, which is the same split the mask model already fits on. CONFOUNDED
  with the loss fix by design; v019 isolates the loss so the two can be attributed.'
change_vs_parent: 'TWO changes off gru_v011. (1) the loss: globally-pooled weighted
  Pearson with a running detached sufficient statistic carried across chunks (stats_window
  = one sequence''s worth of chunks), replacing the per-chunk statistic that was restarted
  every chunk despite a docstring claiming otherwise. (2) data: data.train_shards
  cache/train -> cache/trainval, which concatenates the 10,607 train sequences with
  validation 200..1872 (12,280 sequences, 245,600,000 rows, built and byte-verified
  by tools/make_trainval_shard.py).

  Unchanged: the soft mask, state_reset 4000, chunk_len 2000, aux weighted-MSE 0.05,
  EMA 0.999, architecture 2x128 proj0, seed 1337, 3-epoch cosine schedule. The soft
  mask moves with the data: loss.mask_model cache/train_scored.npy -> cache/trainval_scored.npy,
  because the predicted mask only covers the 212,140,000 train rows. For the 1,673
  added validation sequences is_scored is KNOWN, so those rows get the TRUE indicator
  rather than a prediction -- the same split dozken uses (''validation sequences in
  training use their real mask'').'
